::JOUR_U01_L01_GP_Q01::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which journalism format best reports one timely school announcement in a short factual paragraph? {
~Feature story#Incorrect. A feature usually gives more background and detail than one short announcement needs.
=News brief#Correct. A news brief reports timely facts quickly and directly for the audience.
~Opinion column#Incorrect. An opinion column argues a viewpoint, but the situation asks for factual reporting.
~Photo caption#Incorrect. A caption explains an image, not a full announcement by itself.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_GP_Q02::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Which rule matters most for a photo caption? {
~Include the writer's personal reaction#Incorrect. A caption should identify and explain the image, not center the writer's reaction.
~Use a long introduction before the facts#Incorrect. Captions are usually concise and direct.
~Make a claim about what readers should believe#Incorrect. That rule fits opinion writing more than caption writing.
=Identify who or what is shown accurately#Correct. A caption must help the audience understand the image with accurate identification.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_GP_Q03::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
Why should a news brief avoid unsupported opinion? {
=It must report facts clearly so readers can trust the information.#Correct. News brief quality depends on factual, direct language.
~It should sound more dramatic than other formats.#Incorrect. Drama is not the goal of a factual news brief.
~It needs to hide where information came from.#Incorrect. Journalism should make information clear and accountable.
~It should persuade readers to agree with the writer.#Incorrect. Persuasion is the purpose of opinion or editorial writing, not a news brief.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_GP_Q04::MLA.JOUR.NEWS.06
Which sentence uses the most precise journalism vocabulary? {
~The thing at school is happening soon for people.#Incorrect. This wording is vague and does not help the audience understand the event.
~A cool event is coming and it should be fun.#Incorrect. This sentence uses opinion language and lacks precise details.
=The student council will host a campus cleanup on Friday at 3:30 p.m.#Correct. The sentence gives specific, factual information with precise language.
~Everybody knows the cleanup is a great idea.#Incorrect. This is unsupported opinion, not precise journalistic wording.
}

::JOUR_U01_L01_GP_Q05::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which choice best explains why journalism formats change over time? {
~Formats change only because writers get bored.#Incorrect. Format change is usually connected to audience needs, media tools, and technology.
=New media tools change how audiences receive text, images, audio, and video.#Correct. Technology can change how journalism is created and published.
~Formats change because accuracy is no longer important.#Incorrect. Accuracy remains central even when formats change.
~New formats remove the need for audience awareness.#Incorrect. New formats make audience awareness more important, not less.
}
